# CODEOWNERS — auto-assigns reviewers and gates merge for protected paths.
#
# SHIPPED INERT, ON PURPOSE. Every rule below is commented out.
#
# A CODEOWNERS file containing placeholders — `@[org]/core`, `@your-team` — is
# not a partially-configured gate. `[` and `]` are not legal in a GitHub handle,
# so GitHub reports the whole file as malformed, assigns no owner to any path,
# and the "Require review from Code Owners" rule enforces nothing while the
# settings document goes on claiming it is on. A gate that exists only in prose
# is worse than no gate: it is counted as protection by everyone who reads about
# it and by nobody who relies on it.
#
# To enable, in ONE change: uncomment the rules you want with real handles,
# confirm each resolves (GitHub shows an error banner on this file's page if one
# does not), and turn on "Require review from Code Owners" in main's ruleset.
# Turning either on without the other is the failure above. Note that on a
# single-maintainer repo this rule routes review of the author's work back to
# the author, which is a rubber stamp — leave it off until a second reviewer
# exists.
#
# Syntax: <pattern> <@user-or-team> [<@user-or-team>...]
# More specific lines override less specific ones.

# Default — everything needs review by core
# * @your-org/core

# The operating system itself — changes need senior sign-off
# /CLAUDE.md            @your-org/staff
# /.claude/             @your-org/staff
# /.github/             @your-org/staff
# /docs/decisions/      @your-org/staff

# Specs — product + engineering
# /docs/specs/          @your-org/core @your-org/product

# Migrations — extra eyes on irreversible work
# /migrations/          @your-org/staff
# /db/migrations/       @your-org/staff

# Evals — quality gate, owned by staff
# /evals/               @your-org/staff
